# The decision to publish this repository's OpenSSF Scorecard, and everything that decision sends.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml` sets `publish_results: true`, and that
# one line sends data about this repository to a public third-party endpoint. A line of YAML is not
# where a disclosure decision can live: nothing reads it, and a reader cannot tell a deliberate
# choice from a copied example. `cargo xtask check-workflows` requires this file to exist and to
# carry content whenever that input is true, so the decision is written down or the gate fails.
#
# THE DECISION, dated. Publication is ON, chosen on 2026-09-07, because the README badge was asked
# for and the badge does not render without it - the action's own documentation names
# `publish_results: true` and `id-token: write` as its two prerequisites. Reverting is one line:
# set the input `false`, and the gate then fails until the badge is removed from `README.md`.
#
# WHAT THE PUBLICATION SENDS, AND TO WHOM:
#
#   * to `https://api.scorecard.dev` - the aggregate score, every check's score and its reason
#     text, the repository name, and the commit the run scored. Authenticated by a short-lived
#     OIDC token whose subject names this repository and ref. From there the score reaches the
#     public OpenSSF dataset and the badge.
#
# WHAT A RUN SENDS EVEN WITHOUT PUBLISHING, because the two are separate and only one is optional.
# The action runs every check it has and takes no check-set argument, so these are not selectable:
#
#   * `Vulnerabilities`    -> OSV, at `osv.dev`. It sends the dependency set, not only the name.
#   * `CII-Best-Practices` -> the OpenSSF Best Practices badge API, at `bestpractices.dev`.
#   * `Fuzzing`            -> the OSS-Fuzz project list.
#
# Read against the scorecard 5.x check documentation on 2026-09-07. `nix run .#deny` inside the
# REQUIRED `ci` job already reads advisories against the RustSec database, so the first of those
# duplicates a control this repository has rather than adding one.
#
# THE STATE OF THE REPOSITORY WHEN THIS WAS DECIDED: private, as read on 2026-09-07 from
# `gh api repos/telekom/sutura` (`private: true`, `advanced_security: disabled`). So today the
# badge renders nothing useful and the publication has little to disclose; both become real when
# the repository is made public, which is the point at which this decision starts to matter.
# Measured the same day: the badge URL answers HTTP 302 to a shields.io render, and the REUSE
# badge answers HTTP 200 with the word `unregistered` - api.reuse.software cannot clone a private
# repository.
#
# WHAT NO GATE HERE HOLDS: that this file is TRUE. It is a dated statement about a third party's
# behaviour, and the authority is that third party at the version the action pins. What is
# mechanised is only that it exists, is not empty, and agrees with the badge - see
# `xtask/src/workflows/scorecard.rs` for each rule and the limit beside it.
#
# AMENDED 2026-09-08, and one part of the amendment is a disclosure this record got wrong.
#
# THE PUBLICATION WAS NOT CONDITIONAL ON VISIBILITY. Measured from run 34174327175's log: the
# action prints `Private repository: true` and `Publication enabled: false` and publishes anyway,
# because `main.go` branches on the raw `INPUT_PUBLISH_RESULTS` variable rather than on the
# visibility-adjusted value `options.go` computes (a field with no `env:` tag, so that printed line
# always reads `false` - it is not a control). So while this repository was PRIVATE, three runs:
#
#   * signed the score into the PUBLIC sigstore transparency log - an immutable record, whose
#     certificate names this repository and the workflow ref. `tlog entry created with index:
#     2754066664` is one of the three. That cannot be withdrawn;
#   * POSTed the score, every check's reason, the repository name and the commit to
#     `api.scorecard.dev`, which REFUSED all three with HTTP 400.
#
# The sentence above - "today the publication has little to disclose" - was therefore wrong about
# when the disclosure began. The score itself did not reach the OpenSSF dataset, but the signing
# did reach a public log.
#
# THE REFUSAL, and it was never about being private. `api.scorecard.dev` verifies the producing
# workflow first (`verifyScorecardWorkflow` in `ossf/scorecard-webapp`, read at
# `9c2f66d5f6ff56ca4a4ac2fba6ec8dcc5379d31c`), and `runs-on: rust-mcp` is not on its allowlist of
# runner labels. The action retried, logged `::warning::Unable to POST scorecard results to webapp`
# and returned nil, so the step exited 0. The badge could never have resolved, public or private.
#
# WHAT NOW HOLDS IT: `score` runs on `ubuntu-latest`; `cargo xtask check-workflows` reads this
# workflow against every rule that verification applies, offline and inside the required `ci` job
# (`xtask/src/workflows/publication.rs`); and the `published` job asks the API whether it holds a
# score, failing when it does not. Publication is still ON, for the reason dated 2026-09-07 above.
